  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Torn fibrecap (Inocybe lacera) is a small, delicate mushroom characterized by its distinctive torn or ragged cap surface and fibrous texture. This species is toxic and contains muscarine, a poisonous alkaloid that affects the nervous system.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Torn Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap has radially split or torn fibers creating a characteristic ragged, shaggy appearance that distinguishes it from smoother Inocybe species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown Coloration",
      "description": "The cap ranges from pale brown to reddish-brown, often with darker tones toward the center and visible fibrous streaking.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin Stature",
      "description": "This is a small, fragile mushroom with a thin stem and modest cap diameter (1-3 inches), with gills that are initially whitish then brownish.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Torn fibrecap grows in mixed woodlands, particularly near hardwoods and conifers throughout New England, fruiting in spring and early summer in moist soil.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many Inocybe species, Torn fibrecap has a distinctive earthy or radish-like smell that can help distinguish it from other small brown mushrooms.",
